course: religion-ethics
lesson: 67
title: Grief: anger, guilt and laughing at the wrong moment
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Grief is not only sadness: after his dad's funeral Ben slams doors and shouts «It's not FAIR!», and that anger is part of grief too, because losing someone you love really does feel unfair.
explain: People can be angry at the doctors, at God, at a little sister, even at the person who died; the feeling is allowed, but hurting people is not, so the anger needs a safe way out: running, drawing, punching a pillow, talking.
explain: Guilt is the voice that says «it's my fault»: Lily wonders if her mum got ill because Lily was naughty, and Harvey wishes he had been at Grandad's bedside instead of at football; but illness comes from the body, not from what children do, and love is not cancelled by being somewhere else.
explain: Nervous giggles at a funeral, like Max and his cousin during the prayers, happen to lots of people: the body lets off tension in a strange way, and it does not mean they did not care.
explain: Christians point out that the Psalms are full of angry questions to God, and the vicar says God can take our anger and still loves us; people of every faith and none know these mixed feelings.
explain: For a child today this means: name the feeling, let it out safely, say sorry if it spilled onto someone, and remember that nothing you did caused the death.
fact: Anger is a very normal part of grief; it is okay to feel angry, but not okay to hurt people.
fact: Safe ways to let anger out include running, drawing, punching a pillow and talking to someone.
fact: Doctors try very hard, but some illnesses cannot be cured; it is usually not the doctors' fault.
fact: Many people, even priests, feel angry or confused with God when someone they prayed for dies.
fact: The Psalms in the Bible contain many angry and sad questions to God.
fact: Being naughty never makes anyone ill or die; illness happens because of things in the body.
fact: Death often comes when people are not expecting it, so being away at that moment does not mean you did not love the person.
fact: Nervous laughter at serious times is common; it is the body letting off tension, not a sign of not caring.
fact: Grief can make people snappy; saying sorry helps, and so does telling someone you are sad.
fact: Grief has no time limit; being sad two months later is normal, not «too long».
fact: Love is shown in many ways, not only by saying «I love you».
fact: You can love someone and be angry with them at the same time, even after they have died.
fact: In Australian slang «stoked» means really pleased, «filthy» means really angry, «arvo» means afternoon and «pop» means grandad.
exercise: Why might Ben slam doors after his dad's funeral? | Anger is a normal part of grief | He is just being a naughty, rude boy on purpose | He is secretly glad that his dad has gone away | Doors in old houses always slam shut by accident
exercise: What is the rule about anger in grief? | The feeling is okay; hurting people is not | Anger is never allowed in a sad family | It is fine to hit people when you're grieving | You must hide all anger until you're grown up
exercise: Which is a safe way to let anger out? | Running hard or punching a pillow | Breaking a sister's favourite toy | Shouting insults at the doctors | Kicking the neighbour's cat
exercise: Ruby says the doctors who couldn't save her grandma are useless. What is usually true? | They tried hard, but some illnesses can't be cured | They didn't try, because she was old | Doctors can cure every illness if they want to badly enough | Doctors are always the ones to blame when someone dies in hospital
exercise: Aidan says, «I prayed and she still died.» What does the vicar say about being angry with God? | It's okay; God can take it and still loves you | It is a sin that can never be forgiven | Real Christians never feel anything like that | He must stop praying until the anger goes
exercise: Which part of the Bible is full of angry and sad questions to God? | The Psalms | The Ten Commandments | The Christmas story | The book of Genesis
exercise: Harvey was at football when his grandad died. What is true? | Being away doesn't mean he didn't love him | It was all his own fault for choosing football that day | Grandad died because Harvey wasn't there to watch over him | He should never let himself play football again, to make up
exercise: Why did Max get the giggles during the funeral prayers? | Nervous laughter lets off tension at serious times | He thought his grandma's death was really funny, deep down | He didn't care about his grandma | Laughing out loud is the custom at all funerals in England
exercise: Lily wonders if her mum got ill because Lily was naughty. What is true? | No; illness comes from the body, not from naughtiness | Yes; naughty children can make parents ill | Only if she was naughty many times | Nobody can ever know if it was her fault
exercise: Josh shouted at his little sister after Grandad died and now feels bad. What should he do? | Say sorry and tell someone he's feeling sad | Pretend it never happened | Shout even louder, so that she knows how upset he is | Stay right away from his sister for weeks, until he feels better
exercise: Mia's friend says Mia shouldn't be sad any more because her nan died «ages ago», two months back. What is true? | Grief has no time limit | Grief must end after one month | Two months is far too long to be sad | Being sad later means it is fake
exercise: Oliver wishes he'd said «I love you» more to his uncle. What can comfort him? | Love is shown in many ways, and his uncle knew | His uncle went away angry with him | Words are the only way to show love | It is too late to love anyone now
exercise: Brooke from Sydney says, «I'm stoked we went fishing with him last arvo, but I'm still filthy that he's gone.» How does she feel? | Glad about the memory and angry he died | Bored with fishing and glad he left | Dirty and muddy after fishing in the river all afternoon | Angry with the fish they did not manage to catch that day
exercise: Kai is angry at his dad for driving too fast and dying in a crash. Is that okay? | Yes; love and anger can be there together | No; you must never be angry with the dead | No; it means he never loved his dad | Yes, and he should stay angry for ever
exercise: What does guilt in grief usually sound like? | «It's my fault» or «I should have done more» | «I'm hungry» or «I'm bored of school» | «I knew this would happen all along» | «I am happy that they have gone»
exercise: Which feelings can be part of grief? | Sadness, anger, guilt and even relief | Only sadness, and never anything else at all | Only anger, and never any other feeling | Happiness only, starting after the funeral
test: «It's not FAIR!! the doctors are rubbish, they should've fixed her,» shouts Ruby. What's a gentle, true reply? | It does feel unfair; the doctors tried, but some illnesses can't be cured | You're right; the doctors just didn't bother trying to save her | Doctors are paid to let old people go and save the young ones | Stop being so angry, it's only your grandma, and she was very old anyway
test: «Me and my cuz were crackin' up in the prayers, I'm dead, that's well bad innit,» says Max later (crackin' up = laughing, I'm dead = I'm so embarrassed). Best answer? | Nervous laughter happens; it doesn't mean he didn't care | Yes, he has ruined the funeral for ever, and everyone saw | He must have hated his grandma, or he would never have laughed | He should be banned from going to church ever again
test: A Gen Z cousin says, «Josh went full villain arc on his sister, he's lowkey toxic now.» What's really going on? | Grief can make people snappy; he can say sorry | Josh has turned into a bad person for good and won't change back | His sister must have caused it | Snapping like that proves he doesn't miss Grandad at all
test: What would happen if Harvey kept telling himself that Grandad died because he went to football? | He'd carry guilt for something that wasn't his fault | Grandad would come back to life if he stopped playing football | He'd prove he loved Grandad most | Nothing at all; guilt like that always goes away by itself in time
test: The vicar says, very formally: «It is entirely proper to bring one's anger before God; the Psalmists did so repeatedly.» What does he mean? | It's okay to tell God you're angry; the Psalms do it | Anger must be confessed and then punished | Only Psalm-writers are allowed to be angry | Angry people should stay away from church
test: «Your nan croaked ages ago, get over it,» says a kid to Mia, two months after the funeral. What is true? | Grief has no deadline; Mia's sadness is normal | The kid is right; two months is more than enough time for grief | Mia must be faking it for attention | Sad people should stay at home and not come in to school
test: A boy in Boston says, «Kai's wicked mad at his dad, and his dad's dead, that's messed up» (wicked = very). Fair answer? | You can love someone and be angry at them | Being angry at a dead dad is a sin that God won't forgive | Kai must never have loved his dad at all, not really | Kai should keep all of his anger a secret from everyone
test: Myth to check: «If a child is very naughty, it can make their mum ill.» | False; behaviour doesn't cause illness | True; stress from children causes cancer | True, but only if the child is very bad | False, but only for dads, not mums
test: In a cartoon, a hero smashes the whole town in fury after his mentor dies, and everyone cheers. What's the real-life lesson? | Anger is natural, but it must not hurt people | Smashing things up is the right way to grieve for someone | Anger in grief is always heroic | Only cartoon heroes feel angry when someone they love dies
test: «Proper gutted I never said love you to me uncle, it's doin' me head in,» says Oliver from Manchester (gutted = very upset). Best reply? | Love shows in many ways; your uncle knew | Your uncle died thinking you didn't care | It is too late, so stop thinking about it | Say it to his photo or it doesn't count
test: Brooke says she's stoked and filthy at the same time. Is that weird? | No; grief often mixes feelings together | Yes; you can only feel one thing at once | Yes; she must be confused about fishing | No, because she isn't really sad at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises anger occurring in bereavement? | It is a normal component of grief, provided it is expressed without harm to others | It is an abnormal reaction requiring punishment | It is permissible only when directed at medical staff | It is confined to persons without religious faith
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a child who was absent at the moment of a relative's death bears no fault for that death. | Death frequently occurs unexpectedly, and absence does not occasion it | The child was absent for a sufficiently long period of time | Only adults are capable of causing such events to occur | The relative had expressly forbidden the child's attendance at the bedside
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning laughter during a funeral service. | Nervous laughter is a common release of tension and does not indicate indifference | Laughter during prayers demonstrates contempt for the deceased and for the family | Laughter is a prescribed element of every funeral rite in this country, without exception | Laughter at a funeral is invariably a symptom of illness
exam: It is given that a child believes her misbehaviour caused her mother's illness. Ascertain the response that is consistent with the matters taught. | Illness arises from causes within the body and not from a child's conduct | Her belief is correct where the misbehaviour was frequent | Her belief is correct in respect of minor illnesses only | Her belief cannot be confirmed or denied by anyone
